<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!-- The APPROVAL-STATUS LISTING, laid out by JasperReports — the same printed document as
     creed-report/docs/template.jpg and as creed-report's approval-status-export-pdf.html, from a
     .jrxml instead of from Thymeleaf + CSS 2.1.

         +──────────────────────────────────────────────────+
         |  [logo]                                          |   <- pageHeader    (every page)
         |  ────────────────────────────────────────────────|
         |  Approval Status All List                        |   <- title         (once)
         |  ────────────────────────────────────────────────|
         |  Transaction/Deposit Type   Bank Reference   ...  |   <- criteria subreport, 4 per row
         |  —                         —                    |
         |                                                  |
         |  13 Record(s) (Note: This is a filtered table.)   |   <- still the title band
         |  +──────────────+──────────+───────────+───────+  |
         |  | header band (light blue)                    |  |   <- columnHeader  (every page)
         |  | Bulk MEPS | BK26.. | VASA COMPANY 3 ... | Processing
         |  +──────────────+──────────+───────────+───────+  |   <- detail        (per row)
         |                                                  |
         |  Date of Export: ... | Time of Export: ...  (())  |   <- pageFooter    (every page)
         |  Approval Status All List                  seal   |
         |                     1 of 2                        |
         +──────────────────────────────────────────────────+

     WHERE THE TWO ENGINES DIFFER, and why this file is short where the HTML one is long:

     * REPEATING CHROME IS FREE HERE. pageHeader and pageFooter repeat on every page and pageFooter
       sits at the foot of the PAGE, including a last page the content does not fill. The HTML twin
       needs a wrapper table with -fs-table-paginate for the header and a `position: running()`
       element pulled into an @page margin box for the footer, because Flying Saucer offers nothing
       else that reaches the page margin. Four mechanisms were probed there; two work.
     * counter(page) IS NOT SPECIAL. $V{PAGE_NUMBER} evaluated at Report time gives the total, so
       the "N of M" counter is two ordinary text fields and can sit in the same band as the seal.
       In the HTML that counter can only exist inside an @page margin box, a margin box cannot hold
       an image, and two margin boxes cannot stack — which is the whole reason that footer is a
       running element rather than a table foot.
     * FOUR TO A ROW IS A REPORT PROPERTY. The criteria block is a multi-column SUBREPORT
       (columnCount=4, printOrder=Horizontal); the main report has one column and cannot also have
       four. The HTML uses a <table> of percentage cells, because that renderer has no grid.
     * A ROW THAT GROWS. stretchType="RelativeToTallestObject" makes the three short cells match
       the four-line account cell; CSS table cells do that by themselves.
     * THE PAGE BOX IS POINTS, NOT MILLIMETRES. A4 is 595x842pt; the margins below are the HTML's
       14mm/14mm/14mm sides and top, and the deep bottom band it needs for the two-storey footer.

     WHAT IS THE SAME, deliberately: the payload (byte-for-byte the JSON creed-report serves), the
     embedded Noto faces, the logo and the seal (shared at build time — see the pom), the palette
     (#005CB9 brand blue, #DBE5F1 header band, #C9CCD1 rules, #414042 footnote grey, sampled off the
     sample page), and the per-locale typography: the face follows REPORT_LOCALE, and a caption's
     WEIGHT changes with the script (see the conditional styles below).

     Fields come from com.creed.jasper.service.ApprovalStatusPdfService via a FieldDataSource, not
     from bean introspection: the domain is a record, whose accessors JasperReports' own
     JRBeanCollectionDataSource cannot see.

     ELEMENT HEIGHTS ARE A LOCALE PROBLEM, and this is the landmine that cost a debugging session
     here. A text element shorter than the line the FACE needs prints NOTHING — no warning, no
     clipped glyph, no ellipsis; the line simply does not fit vertically and is dropped. The faces
     are not interchangeable in height:

         font size   Noto Sans   Noto Sans SC/TC   Noto Sans Thai
           8pt         10.90         11.58            12.09
           8.5pt       11.58         12.31            12.84
           9pt         12.26         13.03            13.60
          14pt         19.07         20.27            21.15

     So a 12pt-tall caption at 8.5pt is fine in English, blank in Chinese and blank in Thai, and a
     document proof-read in English ships with its title and every criteria caption missing in two
     languages. Every single-line element below is therefore sized at ~1.6x its font size, the Thai
     line plus headroom — not at the Latin line it looked right at.
       (creed-report cannot hit this: a CSS line box grows to fit its content, and its locale
        overlay only has to adjust the leading.) -->
<jasperReport xmlns="http://jasperreports.sourceforge.net/jasperreports"
              xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
              xsi:schemaLocation="http://jasperreports.sourceforge.net/jasperreports
                                  http://jasperreports.sourceforge.net/xsd/jasperreport.xsd"
              name="approval-status"
              pageWidth="595" pageHeight="842"
              columnWidth="515"
              leftMargin="40" rightMargin="40" topMargin="40" bottomMargin="28"
              resourceBundle="i18n/jasper-messages"
              whenNoDataType="AllSectionsNoDetail">

    <!-- THE GLOBAL DEFAULT FACE. isDefault="true" makes this the font every element inherits unless
         it says otherwise — the exact job `body { font-family: ... }` does in the HTML twin.
         "Creed Sans" is one family name that resolves to Noto Sans / Noto Sans Thai / Noto Sans SC /
         Noto Sans TC by REPORT_LOCALE: fonts/creed-fonts.xml declares the family four times, three
         of them restricted to locales, and the engine picks the first that supports the fill's
         locale (so the unrestricted one is declared LAST, or it would always win).
         Identity-H + embedded, for the same reason creed-report registers its TTFs that way:
         without it the CJK and Thai glyphs do not reach the PDF. -->
    <style name="Base" isDefault="true" fontName="Creed Sans" fontSize="9" forecolor="#212529"
           pdfEncoding="Identity-H" isPdfEmbedded="true"/>

    <style name="StatementTitle" style="Base" fontSize="14" isBold="true" forecolor="#005CB9"/>
    <style name="CountLine" style="Base" forecolor="#414042"/>

    <!-- THE TABLE'S LOOK LIVES HERE; ITS SHAPE DOES NOT. The <jr:table> in the detail band gets
         its columns at compile time (com.creed.jasper.dynamic.TableDesigner), and a generated cell
         names one of these styles rather than carrying a colour of its own — which is why the box,
         the padding and the rule under each row are declared here and not in Java. The generator
         adds exactly one thing these cannot: the table's outer left/right rule, which belongs to
         being the first or last column and is not something a style can know. -->
    <style name="TableHeader" style="Base" mode="Opaque" backcolor="#DBE5F1" forecolor="#1F3864"
           isBold="true" vTextAlign="Middle">
        <box leftPadding="8" rightPadding="4">
            <topPen lineWidth="0.5" lineColor="#C9CCD1"/>
            <bottomPen lineWidth="0.5" lineColor="#C9CCD1"/>
        </box>
    </style>
    <style name="TableCell" style="Base" vTextAlign="Middle">
        <box leftPadding="8" rightPadding="4" topPadding="4" bottomPadding="4">
            <bottomPen lineWidth="0.5" lineColor="#E3E6EA"/>
        </box>
    </style>
    <!-- A cell that is several lines of data rather than one wrapped string: the generator gives
         it textAdjust="StretchHeight" and the rest of the row stretches to match. -->
    <style name="AccountCell" style="Base" vTextAlign="Top">
        <box leftPadding="8" rightPadding="4" topPadding="4" bottomPadding="4">
            <bottomPen lineWidth="0.5" lineColor="#E3E6EA"/>
        </box>
        <paragraph lineSpacing="Proportional" lineSpacingSize="1.35"/>
    </style>
    <!-- The status column, in the accent colour like the sample's. -->
    <style name="StatusCell" style="Base" forecolor="#005CB9" vTextAlign="Middle">
        <box leftPadding="8" rightPadding="4" topPadding="4" bottomPadding="4">
            <bottomPen lineWidth="0.5" lineColor="#E3E6EA"/>
        </box>
    </style>

    <style name="FooterLine" style="Base" fontSize="8" forecolor="#414042"/>
    <style name="PageNumber" style="Base" fontSize="8" forecolor="#414042"/>

    <!-- The document name repeated at the foot of every page, so a loose sheet says which document
         it came from. BOLD — except in Thai, where Noto Sans Thai's loops close up at this size.
         This is the same rule creed-report's static/css/locale/th/report-pdf.css carries for
         .statement-footer-title, and the reason that rule cannot be a message key: it is a weight
         per selector, not a string. -->
    <style name="FooterTitle" style="Base" fontSize="8.5" isBold="true" forecolor="#005CB9">
        <conditionalStyle>
            <conditionExpression><![CDATA["th".equals($P{REPORT_LOCALE}.getLanguage())]]></conditionExpression>
            <style isBold="false"/>
        </conditionalStyle>
    </style>

    <!-- The listing's own dataset, and the table's. It declares NO fields: they are the table's
         columns, added at compile time beside them by com.creed.jasper.dynamic.TableDesigner — a
         field the table has no column for would still be asked of the data source. -->
    <subDataset name="listing"/>

    <parameter name="reportTitle" class="java.lang.String"/>
    <parameter name="note" class="java.lang.String"/>
    <parameter name="exportDate" class="java.lang.String"/>
    <parameter name="exportTime" class="java.lang.String"/>
    <!-- Compiled by JasperReportService and handed in, rather than named as a path the engine's
         repository would resolve during the fill: a broken criteria template then fails when the
         main one does, not on a user's download. -->
    <parameter name="criteriaReport" class="net.sf.jasperreports.engine.JasperReport"/>
    <parameter name="criteriaData" class="net.sf.jasperreports.engine.JRDataSource"/>
    <!-- Classpath names, not streams: a String image expression is loaded through the engine's
         repository and CACHED, so the logo is read once however many pages it is drawn on. A
         stream would be consumed by page one. -->
    <parameter name="logo" class="java.lang.String"/>
    <parameter name="stamp" class="java.lang.String"/>

    <!-- The listing's rows, handed to the table's datasetRun below. The MAIN dataset is filled
         with a single empty record so this band runs once and the table prints once; the rows
         belong to the table, not to the report around it — which is why there are no <field>s
         here at all. -->
    <parameter name="rows" class="net.sf.jasperreports.engine.JRDataSource"/>

    <!-- Bands are declared in the SCHEMA's order (background, title, pageHeader, columnHeader,
         detail, columnFooter, pageFooter, ...), and a band out of sequence fails the schema at
         compile time in a message that names only the element it tripped over.

         THE TITLE BAND PRINTS BEFORE THE PAGE HEADER, not under it. JRVerticalFiller fills title
         then pageHeader, so a logo that lives only in the page header lands BELOW the title — in
         this document, between the record count and the table, which is exactly where it first
         turned up. The fix is the one below: page one's logo is part of the title band, and the
         page header prints from page two on (printWhenExpression). The four lines of <image> are
         duplicated on purpose; the alternative is a document whose first page is upside down.
           (creed-report's twin has the opposite problem and solves it with a wrapper table whose
            <thead> repeats: there, getting a repeated image onto a page at all is the hard part.)

         Everything below belongs to the REPORT rather than to the page and prints once: the logo
         above its hairline, the title fenced between that rule and its own, the criteria block,
         and the record count.

         The subreport stretches with the number of criteria, so the count line under it carries
         positionType="Float" — without it Jasper leaves the element at its designed y and the
         criteria block prints straight over it. -->
    <title>
        <band height="152">
            <image scaleImage="RetainShape" hAlign="Left" isUsingCache="true">
                <reportElement x="0" y="0" width="120" height="31"/>
                <imageExpression><![CDATA[$P{logo}]]></imageExpression>
            </image>
            <line>
                <reportElement x="0" y="39" width="515" height="1"/>
                <graphicElement><pen lineWidth="0.5" lineColor="#C9CCD1"/></graphicElement>
            </line>
            <textField>
                <reportElement style="StatementTitle" x="0" y="47" width="515" height="26"/>
                <textFieldExpression><![CDATA[$P{reportTitle}]]></textFieldExpression>
            </textField>
            <line>
                <reportElement x="0" y="78" width="515" height="1"/>
                <graphicElement><pen lineWidth="0.5" lineColor="#C9CCD1"/></graphicElement>
            </line>
            <subreport isUsingCache="false">
                <reportElement x="0" y="86" width="515" height="40"/>
                <dataSourceExpression><![CDATA[$P{criteriaData}]]></dataSourceExpression>
                <subreportExpression><![CDATA[$P{criteriaReport}]]></subreportExpression>
            </subreport>
            <textField>
                <reportElement style="CountLine" positionType="Float" x="0" y="132" width="515" height="16"/>
                <textFieldExpression><![CDATA[$P{note}]]></textFieldExpression>
            </textField>
        </band>
    </title>

    <!-- The logo, alone, above a hairline, on every page FROM THE SECOND — page one gets it from
         the title band above, because that band prints first (see the note there). No text: the
         document names itself in the title band (once) and in the footer (every page), so
         repeating it here as well would say it three times a page. -->
    <pageHeader>
        <band height="46">
            <printWhenExpression><![CDATA[$V{PAGE_NUMBER} > 1]]></printWhenExpression>
            <image scaleImage="RetainShape" hAlign="Left" isUsingCache="true">
                <reportElement x="0" y="0" width="120" height="31"/>
                <imageExpression><![CDATA[$P{logo}]]></imageExpression>
            </image>
            <line>
                <reportElement x="0" y="39" width="515" height="1"/>
                <graphicElement><pen lineWidth="0.5" lineColor="#C9CCD1"/></graphicElement>
            </line>
        </band>
    </pageHeader>

    <!-- THE LISTING. A <jr:table> component, and the one thing in this file that is not laid out
         here: its <jr:column>s are added at compile time from a List<TableColumn>
         (com.creed.jasper.dynamic.TableDesigner), so the table's SHAPE is data while everything
         around it — page box, chrome, and every style those generated cells name — stays in the
         jrxml where it can be read. A table with no columns is valid on purpose: this is the
         skeleton, and the columns arrive before it is compiled.

         Why the component rather than a generated columnHeader + detail pair: the table owns its
         own pagination and repeats its own caption row per page, so the report keeps ONE ordinary
         detail band and the caption row is the table's business rather than a second band to keep
         in step. The band runs once — the main dataset is a single empty record — and
         splitType="Stretch" lets the table flow onto the next page from inside it.

         The height is a minimum, not the table's: it grows with its rows. -->
    <detail>
        <band height="24" splitType="Stretch">
            <componentElement>
                <reportElement key="listing" x="0" y="0" width="515" height="24"/>
                <jr:table xmlns:jr="http://jasperreports.sourceforge.net/jasperreports/components"
                          xsi:schemaLocation="http://jasperreports.sourceforge.net/jasperreports/components http://jasperreports.sourceforge.net/xsd/components.xsd"
                          whenNoDataType="Blank">
                    <datasetRun subDataset="listing">
                        <dataSourceExpression><![CDATA[$P{rows}]]></dataSourceExpression>
                    </datasetRun>
                </jr:table>
            </componentElement>
        </band>
    </detail>

    <!-- Both storeys of the footnote, in ONE band — the thing the HTML twin cannot do.
         Storey 1: when the report was exported, then its name in bold blue, with the seal floated
         right. Storey 2: the page counter, centred under it. $V{PAGE_NUMBER} evaluated Now is the
         current page and evaluated at Report time is the last one, which is the whole trick; in
         the HTML that counter can only exist inside an @page margin box, a margin box cannot hold
         an image and two of them cannot stack, which is why that footer has to be a running
         element instead.

         THREE fields, not two, and the separator gets its own CENTRED box. The two-field idiom
         ("N" right-aligned | " of M" left-aligned) loses the gap: Jasper's line wrapper strips
         leading whitespace at the start of a line, so " of " renders as "of" and the counter reads
         "1of 2". Centring the word in a box of its own gives the space back on both sides and does
         not care that the separator is " of ", " 页，共 " or " จาก ". The three boxes meet at 237 and 278, either side of 257.5,
         the centre of the content area, so the whole thing stays centred whatever the digits.
         Only pdf.page.middle is used — the sample prints "1 of 2", with no "Page" prefix. -->
    <pageFooter>
        <band height="62">
            <image scaleImage="RetainShape" hAlign="Right" isUsingCache="true">
                <reportElement x="481" y="0" width="34" height="34"/>
                <imageExpression><![CDATA[$P{stamp}]]></imageExpression>
            </image>
            <textField>
                <reportElement style="FooterLine" x="0" y="0" width="440" height="14"/>
                <textFieldExpression><![CDATA[$R{report.statement.exportDate} + " " + $P{exportDate}
                        + "  |  " + $R{report.statement.exportTime} + " " + $P{exportTime}]]></textFieldExpression>
            </textField>
            <textField>
                <reportElement style="FooterTitle" x="0" y="16" width="440" height="15"/>
                <textFieldExpression><![CDATA[$P{reportTitle}]]></textFieldExpression>
            </textField>
            <textField>
                <reportElement style="PageNumber" x="137" y="44" width="100" height="14"/>
                <textElement textAlignment="Right"/>
                <textFieldExpression><![CDATA[String.valueOf($V{PAGE_NUMBER})]]></textFieldExpression>
            </textField>
            <textField>
                <reportElement style="PageNumber" x="237" y="44" width="41" height="14"/>
                <textElement textAlignment="Center"/>
                <textFieldExpression><![CDATA[$R{pdf.page.middle}]]></textFieldExpression>
            </textField>
            <textField evaluationTime="Report">
                <reportElement style="PageNumber" x="278" y="44" width="100" height="14"/>
                <textElement textAlignment="Left"/>
                <textFieldExpression><![CDATA[String.valueOf($V{PAGE_NUMBER})]]></textFieldExpression>
            </textField>
        </band>
    </pageFooter>
</jasperReport>
